<< /Length 1490 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
14 TL
72 720 Td
(Physics-informed and hybrid approaches. SINDy recovers sparse polynomial governing equations) Tj
T*
(directly from data and produces interpretable models that generalize well.) Tj
T*
(Robust losses for time-series. The Huber loss has a long history in robust statistics and has) Tj
T*
(been applied to neural network training to reduce sensitivity to outliers.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3 Problem Setting) Tj
T*
(The competition provides several training trajectories. The task is partitioned into nine evaluation) Tj
T*
(pairs, each specifying training trajectory, initial window, and forecast steps \(1,000 or 10,000\).) Tj
T*
(Two features make the task especially challenging:) Tj
T*
(- Long horizons: Pairs 2 and 4 require 10,000 autoregressive steps.) Tj
T*
(- Heterogeneous regimes: Pairs 6 and 7 use different parameter regimes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(4 Methods) Tj
T*
(Pre-processing and shared pipeline. For each pair the relevant training trajectory is concatenated) Tj
T*
(and standardized. We build supervised pairs with sliding window of length L = 50.) Tj
T*
(\(A\) Vanilla LSTM. A 2-layer unidirectional LSTM with hidden size 128.) Tj
T*
(\(B\) LSTM + Attention. Augments \(A\) with a Bahdanau-style additive attention pooling over time.) Tj
T*
(\(C\) Bidirectional LSTM \(BiLSTM\). Replaces the LSTM in \(A\) with a bidirectional one.) Tj
T*
(\(D\) BiLSTM + Huber loss. Same architecture as \(C\), but trained with the Huber loss with delta = 1.0.) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
endobj
10 0 obj
<< /Length 1504 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
14 TL
72 720 Td
(\(E\) Temporal Convolutional Network \(TCN\). A 4-layer dilated causal CNN with kernel size 3.) Tj
T*
(\(F\) CNN + LSTM. A two-layer 1D CNN front-end followed by a 2-layer unidirectional LSTM.) Tj
T*
(\(G\) CNN + BiLSTM. Identical to \(F\) but with a bidirectional LSTM.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(5 Results) Tj
T*
(Table 1: AI-DEEDS 2026 leaderboard scores \(higher is better; 100 is perfect\):) Tj
T*
(- \(A\) Vanilla LSTM: Score 56.81, Mean Loss 0.0550) Tj
T*
(- \(B\) LSTM + Attention: Score 45.72, Mean Loss 0.1307) Tj
T*
(- \(C\) BiLSTM \(MSE\): Score 58.16, Mean Loss 0.0289) Tj
T*
(- \(D\) BiLSTM + Huber loss: Score 58.81, Mean Loss 0.0149 \(Best\)) Tj
T*
(- \(E\) TCN + Huber: Score 46.55, Mean Loss 0.0272) Tj
T*
(- \(F\) CNN + LSTM + Huber: Score 54.19, Mean Loss 0.0405) Tj
T*
(- \(G\) CNN + BiLSTM + Huber: Score 53.52, Mean Loss 0.0280) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The BiLSTM with Huber loss is the strongest configuration on every metric.) Tj
T*
(Per-pair RMSE on pair 1 \(Lorenz 63 baseline\). Table 2 drills into pair 1.) Tj
T*
(The unidirectional LSTM and the BiLSTM + Huber both achieve perfect single-step prediction.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(6 Discussion) Tj
T*
(Why bidirectional context helps. At first glance, BiLSTMs seem ill-suited to autoregressive forecasting) Tj
T*
(because at inference time there is no future beyond the input window. Within each window, however,) Tj
T*
(the backward pass enriches the representation, helping identify the local dynamical mode.) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
endobj
11 0 obj
<< /Length 1305 >>
stream
BT
/F1 10 Tf
14 TL
72 720 Td
(Why Huber outperforms MSE. Chaotic systems produce occasional large prediction errors.) Tj
T*
(MSE squares such residuals and lets them dominate the gradient. Huber loss caps this influence.) Tj
T*
(Why attention degraded performance. Three factors contribute: short window \(L=50\),) Tj
T*
(attention head/MLP increase overfitting capacity, and Lorenz is near-Markovian \(most recent is best\).) Tj
T*
(Why the CNN front-ends did not help. The CNN parameters increase capacity to overfit and may smooth) Tj
T*
(away the sharp lobe transitions.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(7 Conclusion and Future Work) Tj
T*
(We compared seven LSTM-family forecasters on a chaotic time series benchmark.) Tj
T*
(Bidirectional context combined with Huber loss gives the best leaderboard score \(58.81\).) Tj
T*
(Standard improvements such as attention and CNN front-ends are not universally beneficial.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(References) Tj
T*
([1] E. N. Lorenz. Deterministic nonperiodic flow. Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences, 1963.) Tj
T*
([2] S. Hochreiter and J. Schmidhuber. Long short-term memory. Neural Computation, 1997.) Tj
T*
([3] D. Bahdanau, K. Cho, and Y. Bengio. Neural machine translation. In ICLR, 2015.) Tj
T*
([4] P. J. Huber. Robust estimation of a location parameter. 1964.) Tj
T*
ET

